# Associated sheaves of Cartier divisors

For an integral scheme `X` and a Cartier divisor `D`, this umbrella exports the
construction of `O_X(D)` and its comparison with the Picard group.  It is owned
by three modules:

* `AssociatedSheaf.RationalSections` -- the presheaf of rational functions on an
  integral scheme, as a presheaf of modules over the structure presheaf;
* `AssociatedSheaf.Construction` -- local equations, the fractional subpresheaf
  generated by their inverses, the associated sheaf as its module
  sheafification, its invertibility, and the tensor comparison;
* `AssociatedSheaf.PicardMap` -- the zero, principal, and inverse
  identifications, and the additive map to the Picard group factoring through
  the divisor class group.

## Main definitions

This umbrella declares nothing. The principal exported definitions are
`Scheme.CartierDivisor.associatedSheaf`, the sheaf `O_X(D)`, and
`Scheme.CartierDivisor.classToPicAdd`, the additive map from the divisor class
group to the Picard group.

## Main results

This umbrella proves nothing. Its owning modules supply the tensor comparison
`Scheme.CartierDivisor.associatedTensorAddIso` and the cocycle law
`Scheme.CartierDivisor.equationTransitionIso_trans`.

## Implementation notes

### An intrinsic construction

The fractional presheaf is generated by every local equation.  For two
equations on the same open, `equationTransitionIso` gives the canonical change
of trivialization, and `equationTransitionIso_trans` records its cocycle law.

### No unconditional pullback

No unconditional pullback theorem is stated. `CartierPullbackHypotheses`
supplies a function-field pullback for divisors as an explicit input, but a
comparison with pullback of module sheaves would also need compatibility
between that input, rational sections, and module sheafification. That extra
compatibility is not part of the current Mathlib API.

### Import surface

Importing this module gives the whole associated-sheaf surface; new code should
prefer the narrowest owning module above.

The heavier bridge from `associatedSheaf D` to explicit `LineBundleData`
lives separately in `Divisors.CartierLineBundle`. It is intentionally absent
from this umbrella so effective-divisor consumers do not acquire the
determinant/dual dependency merely by importing the associated-sheaf API.

## References

The owning modules build on Mathlib's `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.functionField`,
`SheafOfModules` and module sheafification, and on this library's
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.CartierDivisor` and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.Pic`.

## Tags

Cartier divisor, associated sheaf, invertible sheaf, Picard group, divisor class group
